# Lesson 3. Serving GraphQL over HTTP.
#
# GraphQL is the document. HTTP is how this process receives it.
# The Express server listens on port 3000. Nest mounts Apollo at /graphql.
#
# POST application/json is the normal request:
#   { "query": "...", "variables": { }, "operationName": "Service" }
# Send one header so a browser form cannot forge the call:
#   apollo-require-preflight: true
# A content-type of application/json also satisfies that check.
#
# GET is for queries only, and only with that header. A mutation on GET is
# rejected even with the header. A GET with no header is rejected as CSRF.
# Use these with an HTTP client, or curl. GraphiQL already sends POST.

### Health is plain HTTP. It is not a GraphQL operation.
GET http://localhost:3000/health

### Query over POST
POST http://localhost:3000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json
apollo-require-preflight: true

{
  "operationName": "Service",
  "query": "query Service { serviceInfo { name graphqlPath transports } }"
}

### The same query over GET. Mutations are not allowed on this method.
GET http://localhost:3000/graphql?query={serviceInfo{name graphqlPath transports}}
apollo-require-preflight: true

### This GET is blocked. No preflight header, no JSON content-type.
GET http://localhost:3000/graphql?query={serviceInfo{name}}

### This mutation is blocked because the method is GET.
GET http://localhost:3000/graphql?query=mutation { deleteBook(id: "1") }
apollo-require-preflight: true

### Two operations in one document. operationName chooses one.
POST http://localhost:3000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "operationName": "Service",
  "query": "query Service { serviceInfo { name } } query Stats { libraryStats { bookCount } }"
}
